llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
139): with a MoE model's routed experts in system RAM, the VRAM left after
the dense weights holds the most-used experts of every layer instead of
every expert of a few layers (--moe-hot / LLAMA_ARG_MOE_HOT). Not
gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803. Patch 141 is its HIP part.

WHY
---
A MoE model too large for the card keeps its routed experts in system RAM,
and llama.cpp's fit fills the VRAM left after the dense weights with whole
layers of experts. One generated token of Qwen3.6 35B-A3B selects 8 of a
layer's 256 experts in each of 40 layers and reads them from RAM on the CPU
(about 0.4 ms per layer on a Ryzen 7 2700X), so the CPU's share of the
token shrinks only by the share of selections that land in the resident
layers. Those layers' experts are no more popular than any others: 8 of 40
layers (3.6 GB) serve 20% of a chat generation's selections.

Routing is skewed within each layer, so the same 3.6 GB spent on the most
often selected (layer, expert) pairs of all layers serves more: 50% of the
selections of chat generations not used to pick them, against 56% for the
best possible set chosen on those generations themselves. What the set is
chosen on matters: a set chosen on plain document text (articles, manuals,
source code) serves 69% of the selections on that same text but only
19-27% on chat generations, because an assistant's answers route to
different experts than the text a chat template wraps.

WHAT
----
- llama_model_params gains moe_hot_path, a text file of "layer expert"
  lines, most-used first (more columns are ignored), and moe_hot_bytes, the
  device memory for them (0: every listed expert). After the weights are
  loaded, the listed experts of the layers whose expert tensors are in host
  memory are read from the model file into one device buffer, until the
  bytes are used up: per layer, tensors of the same type and layout as the
  full ones, packed by slot, plus two tables mapping an expert id to its
  slot or -1, and to itself or -1. They are read from the file because a
  host buffer may hold the full tensors repacked.
- --moe-hot PATH (LLAMA_ARG_MOE_HOT) names the file, or a directory with
  one <model file name>.hot per model; a model without one loads as before.
  --moe-hot-mib (LLAMA_ARG_MOE_HOT_MIB) sets the budget.
- The fit: when a list is given for a single-GPU MoE model that does not fit
  whole and its dense weights, context and compute buffers do fit, all
  routed experts go to system memory and the remaining device memory less
  the fit's margin becomes the budget (or caps --moe-hot-mib). The
  measurement is the one the fit already makes with every expert in host
  memory. When the fit places layers itself (the dense weights and context
  do not fit, or several GPUs), it turns the list off: those layers fill
  the device, and the full list on top of them would not fit. A device
  allocation that fails anyway leaves every expert on the CPU, with a
  warning, instead of failing the load.
- build_moe_ffn, for a one-token batch of a layer with hot experts (no
  expert biases or scales): the selected ids are mapped through both tables
  on the device. The CPU's ids (the cold experts, others -1) are added to
  the graph first, so patch 112's head ends there and the CPU split starts
  while the device runs the hot experts' matrix-vector products from VRAM
  (other ids -1). Each side writes zero rows for the experts it skips, the
  two outputs are added, and the weighting and sum over the experts follow
  as before. Batches of more than one token take the unchanged path: the
  CPU below 32 tokens, the full expert tensors offloaded (patch 108) above.
- ggml-cpu mul_mat_id (both the generic and the repack path) skips an id of
  -1 and writes its output row as zeros.

The CPU and the GPU quantize an expert's input differently (q8_K, q8_1),
so a hot expert's output differs slightly from the CPU's, as it does for
experts in resident layers today. Against all experts on the CPU (llama-
perplexity, 2 chunks of 512, -ub 1), 2,048 hot experts give a mean KLD of
0.0056, the same as moving every expert of the first 8 layers to the GPU
(0.0051); the last 8 layers, which the fit makes resident, give 0.0007:
differences in early layers carry through more layers. PPL 8.52 against
8.58 on the CPU (+-1.02).

Choosing a set: llama-imatrix over chat transcripts (the model's own
answers to typical prompts, formatted with its chat template, run with
--parse-special) stores per-expert selection counts for each layer's
ffn_down_exps; tools/moe_hot_calibrate.py in rocm-gfx803 does all of it for
an Ollama model. A set from imatrix counts serves 48.8% of the held-out
generations against 50.2% from counts taken during generation itself.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590 8 GB driving a KDE desktop, Ryzen 7
2700X, DDR4-2800, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B heretic Q4_K_M, 8k context, q8_0 KV):
- Exactness of the split: with no list, logits byte-identical to the build
  without the patch; with a list holding all 256 experts of layers 32-39
  and every expert in host memory, byte-identical to -ncmoe 32, which keeps
  those layers' experts in VRAM.
- llama-server with its fit, ten chat prompts not used to choose the set,
  400 greedy tokens each, generation tok/s (mean), with patch 139: whole
  layers 33.2, hot experts (3,814 MiB chosen by the fit) 36.6; with HIP
  graphs off 33.8 and 39.2. The unpatched server with whole layers: 31.5.
- Ollama (the image with 139-141 and HIP graphs off), the model's 32k
  context, the same prompts: 28.9 tok/s before, 30.7 without a list, 35.7
  with one (the fit gave 1,857 experts 3,369 MiB); answers coherent, no GPU
  faults.
- Qwen3-Coder-Next heretic (512 experts, 10 per token), llama-server at
  32k context, coding prompts not used to choose the list: whole layers
  22.8 tok/s, hot experts (1,316 in 2,388 MiB) 24.5. At its 256k context
  the dense weights and KV cache do not fit, the fit places layers and
  turns the list off, and the model loads as before.
